{"kind":"report","text":"Approved: exact head d1312c074d1ec26f431f1b03e38b9669f54c0b33, tree 379f692e9d45410438d2c9da7a688a3ad309ab5b, on builder/c4-drafts-20261116, all 71 artifacts, for request 5fc0c5b0 under my promise f6545cf3. This verdict names all three implementations: C4 (request 7555a333, promise 8196ef3a, report 3a7e1ffa, the C4 note, primary), E4-C (request 01c542cc, promise c2692062, report 05bf148d, the act environment note) and XD-1 step 1 (request 9f88af4f, promise 99f12653, report edcc2046, the XD-1 note). `gs review --prepare` resolved all three bindings and reports at this head, with each request closing on the sealed receipt. Main aa72a5b0 is an ancestor.\n\nWhat I read:\n\n- C4, the submit (`scope-client.ts` `submit`, `draft-submit.ts`, `draft-now.ts`). Step 1 copies and checks `slot` and `now` before any wait. `readDraftNow` is a closed reader with exactly six members. The first fenced section applies `taken`, then `state`, then decides from the call's own rows whether the draft is returned. `checkDraftNow` stops with `state` when the view lacks a subject. It then checks the key, kind, address, subject names and primary of `now`. `composeDraftCandidate` builds the candidate from the stored act and the passed `now`, and never from `Explore.result.act`. The exploration runs outside the fence, as ruling 29b355c5 requires. The second section reads the batch again and requires an equal stored draft, or it stops with `state` and writes nothing. A `revision-moved` exploration writes the form 1 return and answers `committed`. Otherwise one batch write adopts the basis, and step 7 signs outside the fence. The cases in `c4-draft-now.test.ts` pin each order point of ruling 09041476 and the three wait cases of 29b355c5.\n- C4, MCP (`service.ts`, `shape.ts`, `input-schema.ts`). `shapeAfter` requires `after` and `now` together, refuses `at` beside them, and requires a new key. It requires `now.of`, target, kind and primary to match the call, and every key of `now.at` to be a bound subject. Then `after` must name a refused record of the same identity and kind in custody. `#nowFor` reads one fresh summary, gives `now` or `lacks` through the shared `lackedSubject`, and stores nothing. `act` reads it only for `revision-moved` and `bad-intent`, and `settle` reads it for every refusal, as ruling 892d1abf sets.\n- E4-C (`replica.ts`). `authorizationSource` keeps each answer in `#sources` by the source entry's hash. A fold again from genesis clears the map. The note now states the bound and the seed start that ruling 01c542cc asked for, and the `d038a2ee` row points to `695a1839`.\n- XD-1 step 1. `guardAbort` wraps `AbortController.prototype.abort` once per isolate. It reports a refused abort only when the reason is Vitest's own timeout message. With `SWALLOW_CROSSING = false` it rethrows, so test behaviour is unchanged. The four Native configs load the setup file, and `gate.sh` prints the count.\n- The version rule. Only the `presentation` IDs of Lending and Counter change, in `release-identifiers.json`, and both applications declare the open version 0.5.0. No signed release file changes. Ruling 163de663 holds.\n- The seals. `security-entry-points.test.mjs` replaces five hashes, for `service.ts` (`$module` and `McpService`), `input-schema.ts`, `act-result.ts` and `expected.ts`. Each one belongs to a file that this head changes, and the pins stage passed on them.\n\nWhat I checked:\n\n- Scope. The 71 artifacts name the head, no path repeats, and their paths equal `git diff --no-renames --name-only aa72a5b0 d1312c07`.\n- Gate 3, END 8aa3daf6. `/Users/hughpyle/play/artroom-evidence/2026-11-16-c4-head-gate-3/gate.log` has SHA-256 722274a4…, `test.log` has 6728c550…, and `crossings.txt` has f34e19d3…. The log names head d1312c07 and tree 379f692e with 0 changed files. Pins passed in 17.3 s. Plain 4957 with 1 skipped and Native 1173 with 2 skipped passed, with 2 crossings, and the last line is \"gate passed\". The evidence directory holds no result file.\n- Cheap checks. The commit-message check passes: 9 checked, 0 flagged. `check-notes` gives 0 warnings. `git diff --check` is clean. I ran no tests.\n\nArchitecture: sound. One rule (`lackedSubject`) decides `lacks` for the client view and for MCP. The client holds no lock across a network wait, and both fenced sections decide on what they read. MCP keeps no draft: `now` comes from a fresh read, and the model passes it back.\n\nSecurity: no blocking finding. `after` and `now` add no authority. The server signs `now.at` unchanged, as it signs a bare `at`, and the scope judges the act. `after` must name a refused request of the same service, membership, signer and target, so a model cannot borrow another identity's record. The XD-1 guard runs only in the test pools.\n\nSimplification: no required change.\n\nPoints for later heads, none blocking:\n\n1. The C4 note says that a `revision-moved` exploration returns the draft. It does not say what the code does for any other refusal: the submit adopts, signs, and lets the scope judge. That fits section 6, because a replica that is `behind` can refuse an act that the scope accepts. State it in the note, so that a reader does not take it for a gap.\n2. Five of the changed seals keep their old reasons, for example \"CE2 adds local custody findings\" on the `service.ts` module. The next head that touches them should say that C4 adds `after`, `now` and `#nowFor`.\n3. The E4-C bound counts rows, but each row holds the revision of every item. The map can grow to the owner's source entries times the item count, until the next fold from genesis. The note should state the size of a row.\n4. The second fenced section answers `state` for every stop of `#acquire`, including `taken`. The ruling allows that. A page then shows the view again in place of the slot's new occupant.\n\nQualifications that stand: gates 1 and 2 on earlier heads FAILED. The two crossings name the test that ran when the timer fired, not its owner; step 1b owns that. No hosted, browser, provider, release or deployment result follows. This approval authorizes no push, landing, signing or deployment. The planner decides the landing.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:3a7e1ffac87d80ab540c13301ca03c0ec18df767","binding":"assigned","head":"d1312c074d1ec26f431f1b03e38b9669f54c0b33","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:f6545cf3c564cd703e330b28df2e5910cae5959d\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:8196ef3a275815e7e3bfdba3821640cfc503b083\",\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:c26920629bf82da372cb69faed64a900a60cfa4f\",\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:99f12653533a3f501e502afc8348d44b96e10b99\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:2b87a3037d04a24ab1ca6624c7b13128c22ed10f","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"approved"}}